Peter H. Salus - UNIX and DEC:  �Snake oil� for Silicon

UNIX was developed on a PDP-7.  It was moved to a PDP-11/24, then ported to
an 11/45, to an 11/70.  Eventually, to a VAX-11/750 and a VAX-11/780.  From
1969 through 1976, UNIX ran on DEC equipment alone; in 1977 it was ported to
the Interdata 7 and the Interdata 8.  The tangled history of DEC and UNIX
will be elaborated.

Peter H. Salus is the author of �A Quarter Century of UNIX� (1994) and
�Casting the Net� (1995) and Editor in Chief of the �Handbook of Programming
Languages�(1998).  He has been Executive Director of the USENIX Association
and of the Sun User Group, Vice President of the Free Software Foundation,
and is currently Director of the Tcl/Tk Consortium.  Peter has appeared on
international television speaking on the history of the UNIX operating
system, and has recently been responsible for the re-printing of the almost
mystical book by John Lyons, "A Commentary on the UNIX Operating System",
first "non-published" twenty years ago.







