From: alain_b@primenet.com (Alain Black)
Subject: Re: Swap: Immediate or Deferred?
Date: 1998/02/13
Message-ID: <34e49cff.94166674@news.primenet.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 324821241
References: <Pine.LNX.3.95.980213112327.8631A-100000@shell.one.net>
Reply-To: alain.black@hfsinc.com
Organization: Cendant
X-Posted-By: @204.160.206.43 (alain_b)
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.misc


The big difference is in the amount of swap that gets used. In
conventional swap mode (over comitment mode) as soon as physical
memory is allocated, the same amount of swap is allocated. This is
ideal incase the system crashes (ie looses power and is not on a UPS),
it will save everything that was in memory on disk (for the most
part). 

Deffered swap (or lazy swap mode) only uses swap when it's needed, so
if you have a lot of physical memory on the machine, this may be the
way to go if the memory is free, and you don't do a lot of paging. If
the system is on a UPS, there is very little chance for losing power
(any problem that is not even remotely possible WILL happen to you and
your system, trust me). 

I use lazy swap on our systems, but we have 2GB of physical ram in the
8400's, and plenty of it free.

Hope this answered your question......


