In article <01bd326d$f660e050$0230c2c3@earth>, "Colin Carpenter" <ccarpenter@planetcs.co.uk> writes:
>Hi,
>
>I wonder if there is anyone out there that can help.... I'm new to Unix and
>am having problems copying an installation from one machine to another.
>Both machines are Digital Alphas 4/233 running DU4.0c.  The problem that
>I'm having is to do with the disk names.  On the original machine they're
>called RE0, RE1 etc and the partitions are RE0a, RE0b etc.  On the new
>machine I can only ever seem to have disks called RZ0, RZ1 etc - so when I
>try and restore the whole system from one machine to the other it won't
>boot  - how do I get them to be RE disks???? Am I missing something
>obvious???
>

Quite often when you install a UNIX, hardware paths to such things as the
swap partition are built into the kernel.  The easiest way for you to "copy"
you UNIX installation may be to install UNIX on the target machine, and then
copy everything else.

