A DIGITAL UNIX PROGRAM LIKE YOU�VE NEVER SEEN BEFORE!

DIGITAL UNIX Tutorials � �All Hands on DEC�

Starting off the program on Sunday, April 5, 1998, we have eight full day
tutorials.  These are being presented by some of the leading instructors in
the industry and many will include hands on hardware to work with.  You are
strongly encouraged to consider signing up for one of these tutorials.  To
encourage you to sign up for these day long technical tutorials, DIGITAL has
provided special sponsorship for these that will allow you to attend at less
than half the normal cost ($195 versus $495).

TU001 Evaluating Storage Options for DIGITAL UNIX
TU002 DIGITAL UNIX TCP/IP Configuration
TU003 Using Clustering for High Availability and Performance with DIGITAL
UNIX � JUMP START
TU004 Windows NT and UNIX Integration Guide
TU005 JUMP START into DIGITAL UNIX System Administration
TU006 Configuring DIGITAL UNIX as a Webserver
TU007 DIGITAL UNIX Networking Internals
TU008 Integrating Applications with the Common Desktop Environment (CDE)


Digital UNIX Sessions � �Under the Covers�

In order to understand the proper way of installing, tuning and otherwise
maintaining the modern-day UNIX system, you first have to understand the
subsystems within it and the "knobs" for tuning it.  What knobs exist?
Where do they exist?  What can you do with them?  How do they affect each
other?  What knobs are coming in future releases?  How will these knobs
affect what I do?

To explain all these very gnarly knobs, are some of the world's best UNIX
engineers.  They are the people who build Digital's UNIX system, and they
will be discussing knobs and many other topics from a systems administration
viewpoint.  No bulleted list of features, but task oriented, solid training
on what is available for installation and tuning by the systems
administrator from the people who implemented the operating system, whether
it be a single system, a Highly Available TruCluster, a High Performance
TruCluster or UNIX-oriented networking.

In addition to all of this, if you are having trouble getting your Advanced
Server product to work properly, or you need to know how to administer
hundreds of PCs on top of your UNIX server and still have hair, this
Conference is the one to attend!

Although we will still have the traditional Birds-of-a-Feather sessions, we
noticed that all most birds do is squawk a lot and flap their wings.  We are
therefore instituting "Hints and Kinks", where engineering will work with
customers to solve knotty problems.  While there is no guarantee that your
problem will be solved, we are committed to have the engineering staff
available to help you meet this goal.







