TU001:  Evaluating Storage Options for Digital UNIX

Instructor: Dean Brock

Mr. Brock is the Chairperson of the University of North Carolina's Computer
Science Department. Dean has had extensive experience consulting for Fortune
500 companies (IBM, DEC, Becton-Dickerson, etc.) in parallel and distributed
computing, distributed systems and computer networking.

The courses Dean has taught include, but are not limited to, "The OSF/1
Operating System", "OSF DCE", "Distributed Systems", "Network Programming",
and "Operating Systems Design". He has been a referee for The Journal for
the ACM, ACM Transactions of Programming Languages and Systems, IEEE
Computer, Computing Surveys, IEEE Transactions on Computers, Information
Processing Letters, and several conferences.

Format:  One Day Lecture/Laboratory

Prerequisites: Attendees should have completed the Digital UNIX System
Administration course or have equivalent experience

Objectives:
Show the capabilities of various storage solutions for Digital UNIX in
meeting competing demands of performance, high availability, cost and ease
of use.  System administrators will learn to which storage options that are
available with Digital UNIX can solve their particular problems.

The session will start with a definition of metrics for evaluation storage
solutions then introduce the main storage related products for Digital UNIX;
The Advanced File System (AdvFS), The Logical Storage Manager (LSM),
Networker Backup and Restore and Hardware RAID subsystems.  The
capabilities, benefits and weaknesses of each product will be discussed.
The course will also cover how products can be used in conjunction with each
to result in a more capable storage solution then when used alone.

Finally, we will spend the afternoon learning to install, configure, and
administer the File Systems using AdvFS and Hardware RAID.







