TU002:  Digital UNIX TCP/IP Configuration

Instructor: Ken Pugh

Mr. Pugh is a columnist for "The C Users Journal", a member of the ANSI
X3J11 C Standards Committee, and an author of "All on C", a C textbook
published by Scott, Foresman and Company.

Ken has taught courses for and consulted with numerous Fortune 500 and
start-up companies on C, C++, UNIX, X-Windows, Motif, DCE, and the OSF/1
operating system. Some of these corporations include Digital Equipment
Corp., Aetna LifeInsurance, Raytheon, Mobil, DSC, State Mutual Life
Assurance, Houghton-Mifflin Publishing, and Lincoln Laboratory.

He has recently been an invited speaker at a number of C and C++ conferences
where he has given the following seminars: "That's an Object? IObject!"; and
"Function and Data Abstraction - Moving to C++".


Format:      One Day Lecture/Laboratory

Prerequisites: UNIX Utilities and Commands
         UNIX System Administration is helpful

Objectives:
This course offers a concise overview of UNIX networking, and networking in
general. You will learn the architecture of TCP/IP and gain an understanding
of the underlying protocols.

During the two hour lab you will configure TCP/IP networking and setup an
NFS service. If time permits (on a student by student basis) an advanced lab
will be offered to export an NFS file system.








