TU004:  Windows NT and UNIX Integration Guide

Instructor:  Dr. William Moloney

Dr. Moloney has been involved with the UNIX operating system both
academically and commercially since 1979, and has participated in several
kernel level development efforts. Dr. Moloney has offered seminars dealing
with the UNIX environment from several perspectives, including those of
shell users, systems programmers, network software developers using TCP/IP,
driver developers and kernel programmers.

He has done extensive development work at the University and with industry
on standalone and on-line diagnostic systems for several hardware and
network platforms. This work has lead to several commercial products in use
today.

Most recently, he has worked with ISA to offer seminars on DCE, DIGITAL UNIX
and X-Windows and Motif. and is the author of ISA�s NT Clusters courseware.
In addition, he has offered more than 60 on-site seminars dealing with the
UNIX environment for various commercial accounts.

Format:  One-day Lecture/Demo JUMP Start

Prerequisites:
This course is valuable for system administrators and managers responsible
for integrating Windows NT and UNIX. Familiarity with UNIX or NT network
administration and TCP/IP protocols is useful.

Objective:
As Windows NT matures and its installed base expands, it is increasingly
Common to find Windows NT deployed alongside UNIX.  Integrating these two
very different operating systems in an enterprise environment poses a
considerable challenge to system administrators, integrators, developers and
managers.

In this unique course, discussion focuses on the real-world issues you face
integrating Windows NT and UNIX.  You gain the skills and experience needed
to begin to provide seamless access to network resources.







