TU005:  JUMP START into DIGITAL UNIX System Administration

Instructor: Jane Montiquila

Jane was employed at  Digital  Equipment  Corporation  from  1982 through
1993,  providing customer training to Digital customers.  As an Educational
Consultant, she developed and provided training for technical courses
including  Operating Systems, Languages and Local and Wide area Networking.
She has  received  many  Professional Awards for her achievements in these
areas.

In 1993 she became an independent consultant, providing  training for  many
different  Hardware and Software vendors.  In 1998 she began her own company
TFSO, Training for Successful Organization, and  continues  to  develop and
provide training in UNIX, OpenVMS and Local and Wide area Networks, as well
as many  PC  platforms.  Educated at Boston State College, in Liberal Arts,
Massasoit Community College, in Business Administration and Dean Jr.
College, in Computer
Science.



Format:    One Day Demonstration/Laboratory

Prerequisites:   Experience in a UNIX environment as a User

Objective:
This is a quick and dirty course that will show you the essentials of
installing a DIGITAL UNIX system correctly. In the process of learning about
and doing an installation the students will get an introduction to a number
of DIGITAL UNIX administrative tasks involving disk and file system
management, kernel configuration, network configuration and user account
management.

Students begin by getting hands-on experience checking hardware and setting
environment variables using the console commands.  In the next lecture/lab
session will learn to partition, create, and mount UFS (the UNIX File
System). The essentials of system startup and shutdown are discussed as well
as bootstrapping a system. Sessions on Kernel configuration and using the
LMF (License Management Facility) are covered.  The course ends with a
session on creating and managing users and groups.








