TU007:  DIGITAL UNIX Networking Internals

Instructor:  Dr. Thomas W. Doeppner, Jr.

Dr. Doeppner has been a Member of the Institute for Advanced Professional
Studies since 1984.  Considered an authority on operating systems,
distributed computing, and multithreaded programming, he has prepared and
presented numerous OS internals courses (UNIX System V, 4.x BSD, ULTRIX,
NFS), distributed computing courses (DCE), and threads courses (Solaris,
POSIX).  Dr. Doeppner was the principal developer of the first courses on
the internals of the OSF/1 operating system and on the internals of DCE
(courses developed by IAPS for the Open Software Foundation), and the first
OSF/1 Microkernel course.

Dr. Doeppner has been on the Computer Science faculty of Brown University in
Providence, RI since 1976 and is currently a Research Associate Professor.
He has taught in a variety of areas including operating systems, programming
languages and distributed computing.  He managed the Brown Computer Science
Department's computing facilities for a number of years.

He has presented numerous tutorials at USENIX and other leading conferences
worldwide since 1986.

B.Sc. (with distinction), Computer Science, Cornell University
M.A., Ph.D., Computer Science, Princeton University

Format: Interactive Lecture

Audience:  System Network Administrators interested in a quick but
detailed introduction to networking in Digital UNIX

Prerequisites: Knowledge of DIGITAL UNIX internals is helpful but not
necessary.

Objective:
This seminar provides a comprehensive treatment of the Digital UNIX
implementation of networking.  It describes how TCP/IP and related protocols
work and how the protocols are implemented in Digital UNIX.  We will examine
the requirements for each of the protocols and their implementations,
discuss performance issues and make some comparisons with other
implementations of networking such as the purely streams-based approach
found in SVR4-based systems.








