TU008:  Integrating Applications with the Common Desktop Environment (CDE)

Instructor:  Robert C. Seacord, Software Engineering Institute at CMU

Robert Seacord is a Member of the Technical Staff at the Software
Engineering Institute at Carnegie Mellon University. As a Member of the
Technical Staff at the Institute for Advanced Professional Studies in
Cambridge, Massachusetts, he develops courseware on CDE Application
Integration and Administration. Previously, he was a developer of Version
2.1 of CDE and Motif at the X Consortium in Cambridge.

Mr. Seacord has over 15 years of software development experience in
industry, defense and research. He has worked extensively with UNIX, Motif,
CDE, and other graphical user interface systems and technologies. Mr.
Seacord's principal areas of expertise include graphical interface design,
human factors and component-based software engineering.

Prerequisites:  System Administrators with basic UNIX experience.

Objective:
The Common Desktop Environment (CDE) is an integrated graphical user
interface and suite of end-user applications for open systems desktop
computing.  Far more than a window manager, CDE offers integrated utilities
and applications such as a calendar, e-mail, a graphical Korn Shell, and
inter-process coordination capabilities.  CDE provides end users with a
graphical means of locating and starting custom developed or third party
applications on the desktop, the ability to recognize and manipulate these
applications' data files, and an easy, standard mechanism for font and color
selection using CDE's Style Manager.  This seminar is intended for System
Administrators who need to install and integrate their applications with the
desktop.

The Tutorial Covers:
? Understanding the CDE Architecture and Features
? Adding and Administering Applications
? Customizing the Desktop using CDE's Create Action
? Integrating the Application using CDE's Create Action
? Supporting printing for the application's data files
? Registering the Application with the Application Manager
? Adding Drag and Drop using the Action System
? Administering Application Resources, Fonts, and Colors







